WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.020
The line went live last week.

2
00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.280
Quiet callers are fine.

3
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.700
Loud callers hear a harsh, raspy buzz.

4
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.980
One says his boss sounds like
he's yelling through a kazoo.

5
00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:10.450
Strange, isn't it?

6
00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.150
Same code for everybody,
and louder should just be louder.

7
00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.010
The line carries the microphone's voltage,

8
00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.850
measured eight thousand times a second.

9
00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:19.550
Each is a sample; the list is a signal.

10
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.090
The simplest sound is a tone.

11
00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.590
How often it repeats,
in hertz, is its pitch.

12
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.450
Its samples are the cosine
of an angle that turns a fixed step:

13
00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.290
at one thousand hertz, forty-five degrees.

14
00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:30.420
Eight samples make a full turn.

15
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.960
The booster is a system:
one signal in, another out.

16
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.080
Its rule: multiply every sample by four.

17
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.080
But the chip after it only stores
the range, minus one to plus one.

18
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.160
Hand it two, past the edge,
and it writes one.

19
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.640
That's clipping.

20
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.780
A quiet caller is size point two,

21
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:46.720
their biggest sample;
a loud one, point five.

22
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.860
Which comes out clean?

23
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.460
The quiet one grows to point eight.

24
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.740
The loud one would reach two:
its tops get sliced flat.

25
00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:54.510
Is that just an uglier tone?

26
00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:55.450
Try the neighbor rule:

27
00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.570
in this tone, any two neighbors add
up to one point four one four times

28
00:00:58.590 --> 00:00:59.950
the sample between them.

29
00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:01.070
The clean tone passes.

30
00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.950
The clipped one fails.

31
00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:05.110
So what is it?

32
00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:07.310
It's two tones: a one thousand hertz tone,

33
00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.370
minus a three thousand hertz
tone about a sixth its size.

34
00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:11.870
That second tone is the buzz.

35
00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.330
The booster made it, out of nothing.

36
00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.090
What did the booster break?

37
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:19.030
A system is linear when doubling
the input doubles the output,

38
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.050
and inputs added give outputs added.

39
00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:22.590
The real booster is not linear.

40
00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:27.160
It only behaves like the linear times four
while its outputs stay inside the range.

41
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:27.860
Here's the trap.

42
00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.680
Two quiet voices share a speakerphone.

43
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.600
Each alone peaks at point eight.

44
00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.260
Together, linearity says one point six;
the chip writes one, and the pair buzzes.

45
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.740
Linearity is a promise about sums.

46
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.740
Time-invariant means delaying
the input only delays the output.

47
00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.700
The smoother, averaging each sample
with the one before, has both.

48
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.540
Any system with both properties
keeps a tone's pitch:

49
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.020
it can only resize and shift it.

50
00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.000
No new tones, ever.

51
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.020
The fix:
keep every output inside the range.

52
00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.560
The loudest input,
speakerphones included, is point five.

53
00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.220
Times two lands right on the edge.

54
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.320
Instead, keep the loudest peak
at point eight, a fifth below the edge:

55
00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.080
that's headroom.

56
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.260
So: times one point six.

57
00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.590
The buzz meter,
counting new tones, reads zero.

58
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:12.060
Loud callers sound like loud people again;
quiet ones peak at point three two,

59
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.100
quieter but clean.

60
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:14.840
Then a new ticket: I can hear myself.

61
00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.340
Every word comes back, faint,
a quarter second later.

62
00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.000
That's the next bug.
